  "publication_text": "Dark blossoms and broad leaves float beside reflected clouds on the \nsurface of Claude Monet’s pond. Scribbling, scrawling strokes of paint \nat once conjure the plants and dissolve their forms into pure color, \na single, continuous veil of green and pink, blue and white. Datable by \nits palette and handling to the mid-1910s, this joyous, serene composition betrays no hint of the First World War raging not far away. \nA founding member of the Impressionist movement, Monet had \nportrayed flowers and gardens, and had championed the practice of \npainting en plein-air (outdoors), since the beginning of his career. \nIn 1883, he and his family moved to Giverny, a small town fifty miles \nnorthwest of Paris along the river Seine. Over the next two decades, \nhe proceeded to buy not only a house and garden there but also a plot \nof land across the railroad tracks, where he developed a small natural \npond into an extensive Japanese water garden, planted with exotic \nspecies and modeled on gardens in the colorful woodblock ukiyo-e \nprints that he collected. \nThis environment furnished Monet with inspiration for some \ntwo-hundred-fifty pictures, painted both outdoors and in his studio, \nwhere, in the final decades of his life, he worked surrounded by his own \nimages of the water-lily pond. The San Francisco Water Lilies appears \nto be unfinished. As in many of Monet’s paintings, the composition \ntapers out around the margins—except for the left edge, where a considerably less finished portion of the canvas may have been cut away by \nthe artist’s son Michel after Monet’s death. EAB",
